1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,893
<font color="#FFFFFF">Transformer-based language models show a striking
ability called in-context learning,</font>

2
00:00:05,973 --> 00:00:12,654
<font color="#FFFFFF">where a pretrained model solves brand-new tasks
just by seeing a few input-output examples in its prompt,</font>

3
00:00:12,734 --> 00:00:13,941
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no fine-tuning.</font>

4
00:00:14,021 --> 00:00:18,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">But why training produces this ability, and how
well it generalizes,</font>

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:24,887
<font color="#FFFFFF">has stayed mostly a mystery because the math of
nonlinear attention and nonlinear activations is hard.</font>

6
00:00:24,967 --> 00:00:30,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">This ICML 2024 paper gives the first theoretical
analysis of the training dynamics of</font>

7
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:34,803
<font color="#FFFFFF">a Transformer with both nonlinear self-attention
and a nonlinear MLP,</font>

8
00:00:34,883 --> 00:00:38,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">and proves when the trained model generalizes in-context,</font>

9
00:00:38,553 --> 00:00:41,306
<font color="#FFFFFF">both in-domain and under distribution shift.</font>

10
00:00:41,386 --> 00:00:46,264
<font color="#FFFFFF">It also delivers the first theory of how pruning
affects in-context learning,</font>

11
00:00:46,344 --> 00:00:50,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that removing the small-magnitude neurons
barely hurts.</font>

12
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:56,346
<font color="#FFFFFF">In-context learning lets a pretrained Transformer
handle new tasks by simply padding the</font>

13
00:00:56,426 --> 00:00:59,664
<font color="#FFFFFF">query with a handful of example input-output pairs,</font>

14
00:00:59,744 --> 00:01:01,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">no fine-tuning required.</font>

15
00:01:01,305 --> 00:01:03,177
<font color="#FFFFFF">Despite its empirical success,</font>

16
00:01:03,257 --> 00:01:08,317
<font color="#FFFFFF">the mechanics of how a Transformer is actually
trained to acquire this ability,</font>

17
00:01:08,397 --> 00:01:11,766
<font color="#FFFFFF">and how far that ability generalizes, remain elusive.</font>

18
00:01:11,846 --> 00:01:17,751
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core difficulty is technical: the
self-attention layer is nonlinear through the softmax,</font>

19
00:01:17,831 --> 00:01:21,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the MLP is nonlinear through the ReLU activation,</font>

20
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:26,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the training problem is nonconvex and resists
the tools used for simpler models.</font>

21
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:30,118
<font color="#FFFFFF">Recent works began to explain in-context learning,</font>

22
00:01:30,198 --> 00:01:32,693
<font color="#FFFFFF">but each keeps only part of the picture.</font>

23
00:01:32,773 --> 00:01:35,331
<font color="#FFFFFF">Some ignore the nonlinear self-attention,</font>

24
00:01:35,411 --> 00:01:38,614
<font color="#FFFFFF">others replace the nonlinear MLP with a linear one,</font>

25
00:01:38,694 --> 00:01:42,475
<font color="#FFFFFF">and most study linear regression rather than
classification.</font>

26
00:01:42,555 --> 00:01:50,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">None can characterize how to train a model that
generalizes under a distribution shift between training and test data,</font>

27
00:01:50,150 --> 00:01:54,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">and none analyze how pruning a trained model
changes its in-context ability.</font>

28
00:01:55,042 --> 00:02:00,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">Since practitioners routinely prune large language
models to save compute while hoping</font>

29
00:02:00,577 --> 00:02:02,556
<font color="#FFFFFF">to keep their in-context skills,</font>

30
00:02:02,636 --> 00:02:05,710
<font color="#FFFFFF">closing this theoretical gap matters in practice.</font>

31
00:02:06,090 --> 00:02:09,981
<font color="#FFFFFF">This work makes three contributions.</font>

32
00:02:10,061 --> 00:02:13,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

33
00:02:14,032 --> 00:02:17,923
<font color="#FFFFFF">it is the first theoretical characterization of how
to train a Transformer that keeps</font>

34
00:02:18,003 --> 00:02:21,894
<font color="#FFFFFF">both nonlinear self-attention and a nonlinear MLP,</font>

35
00:02:21,974 --> 00:02:25,865
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it proves the trained model generalizes in
context to unseen tasks,</font>

36
00:02:25,945 --> 00:02:29,835
<font color="#FFFFFF">quantifying the required amount of data, number of
iterations, and context length.</font>

37
00:02:29,915 --> 00:02:33,806
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it expands our understanding of the
mechanism of in-context learning,</font>

38
00:02:33,886 --> 00:02:37,777
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing how the attention layer and the MLP layer
cooperate to make correct predictions.</font>

39
00:02:37,857 --> 00:02:41,748
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

40
00:02:41,828 --> 00:02:45,719
<font color="#FFFFFF">it provides the first theoretical analysis of
magnitude-based pruning for in-context learning,</font>

41
00:02:45,799 --> 00:02:49,690
<font color="#FFFFFF">proving that removing low-magnitude neurons is
essentially harmless.</font>

42
00:02:50,070 --> 00:02:54,201
<font color="#FFFFFF">The model is deliberately minimal but nonlinear:
one self-attention head with a softmax,</font>

43
00:02:54,281 --> 00:02:58,412
<font color="#FFFFFF">followed by a two-layer perceptron with ReLU activation.</font>

44
00:02:58,492 --> 00:03:02,622
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training augments each query with a length-l
context of example pairs,</font>

45
00:03:02,702 --> 00:03:06,833
<font color="#FFFFFF">embeds them into a prompt,</font>

46
00:03:06,913 --> 00:03:11,044
<font color="#FFFFFF">and minimizes a hinge loss over prompts sampled
from a small subset of binary classification tasks.</font>

47
00:03:11,124 --> 00:03:15,255
<font color="#FFFFFF">Every input contains relevant patterns that
determine its label and irrelevant patterns that do not.</font>

48
00:03:15,335 --> 00:03:19,465
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors follow the gradient-descent dynamics of
the query, key, value,</font>

49
00:03:19,545 --> 00:03:23,676
<font color="#FFFFFF">and MLP weights,</font>

50
00:03:23,756 --> 00:03:27,887
<font color="#FFFFFF">and evaluate the resulting model both in-domain and
under a distribution shift where the</font>

51
00:03:27,967 --> 00:03:32,098
<font color="#FFFFFF">test relevant patterns are linear combinations of
the training ones.</font>

52
00:03:32,178 --> 00:03:36,308
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two quantities drive the guarantees: alpha,</font>

53
00:03:36,388 --> 00:03:40,519
<font color="#FFFFFF">the fraction of context examples that share the
query's relevant pattern, and beta,</font>

54
00:03:40,599 --> 00:03:44,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">the magnitude of the relevant features.</font>

55
00:03:45,110 --> 00:03:49,472
<font color="#FFFFFF">Experiments use a controlled synthetic setup that
matches the theory.</font>

56
00:03:49,552 --> 00:03:54,235
<font color="#FFFFFF">There are six in-domain relevant patterns and
twenty-four irrelevant ones,</font>

57
00:03:54,315 --> 00:03:58,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">and tasks are binary classifications built from
these patterns.</font>

58
00:03:58,370 --> 00:04:03,118
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two models are trained: the one-layer Transformer
that the theory analyzes,</font>

59
00:04:03,198 --> 00:04:07,045
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a small real-world GPT-2 with three layers and
two heads.</font>

60
00:04:07,125 --> 00:04:12,903
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training uses a context length of twenty and a
relevant-pattern fraction of eighty percent,</font>

61
00:04:12,983 --> 00:04:20,434
<font color="#FFFFFF">and out-of-domain evaluation uses new tasks whose
relevant patterns are linear combinations of the training patterns,</font>

62
00:04:20,514 --> 00:04:22,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">exactly the regime the theorems cover.</font>

63
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:28,378
<font color="#FFFFFF">The main theorem shows that a number of iterations,
and a matching number of samples,</font>

64
00:04:28,458 --> 00:04:33,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">that grow only polynomially in the problem
parameters is enough to drive the in-domain</font>

65
00:04:33,716 --> 00:04:36,266
<font color="#FFFFFF">generalization error down to order epsilon.</font>

66
00:04:36,346 --> 00:04:41,280
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, the same trained model also generalizes
out of domain to shifted tasks,</font>

67
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:46,477
<font color="#FFFFFF">as long as the new relevant patterns are linear
combinations of the training patterns</font>

68
00:04:46,557 --> 00:04:49,046
<font color="#FFFFFF">with coefficients summing to at least one.</font>

69
00:04:49,126 --> 00:04:54,427
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments confirm this sharply: the
out-of-domain classification error falls below</font>

70
00:04:54,507 --> 00:04:58,585
<font color="#FFFFFF">one percent exactly when the combination strength
S-one reaches one,</font>

71
00:04:58,665 --> 00:05:00,358
<font color="#FFFFFF">and stays near zero above it,</font>

72
00:05:00,438 --> 00:05:05,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the required context length and iterations
track the predicted dependence on alpha.</font>

73
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:10,326
<font color="#FFFFFF">Ablations vary the fraction of context examples
that share the query's relevant pattern.</font>

74
00:05:10,406 --> 00:05:14,552
<font color="#FFFFFF">As predicted,</font>

75
00:05:14,632 --> 00:05:18,778
<font color="#FFFFFF">the needed context length grows like one over alpha
and the needed iterations and samples</font>

76
00:05:18,858 --> 00:05:23,004
<font color="#FFFFFF">grow like alpha to the minus two-thirds,</font>

77
00:05:23,084 --> 00:05:27,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">so richer contexts converge faster and need shorter
prompts.</font>

78
00:05:27,310 --> 00:05:31,456
<font color="#FFFFFF">A second comparison pits in-context learning
against logistic regression,</font>

79
00:05:31,536 --> 00:05:35,682
<font color="#FFFFFF">kernel and linear SVMs, and nearest-neighbor classifiers.</font>

80
00:05:35,762 --> 00:05:39,908
<font color="#FFFFFF">When the relevant fraction is high the gap is small,</font>

81
00:05:39,988 --> 00:05:44,134
<font color="#FFFFFF">but in the harder low-fraction regime in-context
learning is the most sample-efficient method,</font>

82
00:05:44,214 --> 00:05:48,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">indicating it removes irrelevant data and tolerates
label noise better than the classical baselines.</font>

83
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:51,337
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the paper's message.</font>

84
00:05:51,417 --> 00:05:54,715
<font color="#FFFFFF">The training cost, both in iterations and in samples,</font>

85
00:05:54,795 --> 00:05:57,265
<font color="#FFFFFF">scales as alpha to the minus two-thirds,</font>

86
00:05:57,345 --> 00:06:00,451
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the required context length as one over alpha.</font>

87
00:06:00,531 --> 00:06:04,212
<font color="#FFFFFF">The model needs to be trained on only a vanishing
fraction,</font>

88
00:06:04,292 --> 00:06:09,375
<font color="#FFFFFF">order one over the square root of the number of
patterns, of all in-domain tasks,</font>

89
00:06:09,455 --> 00:06:11,351
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet it generalizes to the rest.</font>

90
00:06:11,431 --> 00:06:16,832
<font color="#FFFFFF">Out-of-domain error drops below one percent once
the combination strength reaches one.</font>

91
00:06:16,912 --> 00:06:22,441
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on pruning: removing the low-magnitude neurons
keeps error at order epsilon plus one</font>

92
00:06:22,521 --> 00:06:24,353
<font color="#FFFFFF">over the square root of M-one,</font>

93
00:06:24,433 --> 00:06:25,692
<font color="#FFFFFF">essentially lossless,</font>

94
00:06:25,772 --> 00:06:31,619
<font color="#FFFFFF">and in experiments magnitude-based pruning of up to
about fifteen percent of the output-layer</font>

95
00:06:31,699 --> 00:06:34,487
<font color="#FFFFFF">neurons leaves out-of-domain accuracy intact,</font>

96
00:06:34,567 --> 00:06:40,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas pruning the large-magnitude neurons
degrades error at least linearly in the pruning rate.</font>

97
00:06:41,050 --> 00:06:46,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message is that in-context learning in
a nonlinear Transformer is not a black</font>

98
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:49,982
<font color="#FFFFFF">box: with both nonlinear attention and a nonlinear MLP,</font>

99
00:06:50,062 --> 00:06:54,175
<font color="#FFFFFF">a one-layer model can be provably trained to
generalize in context,</font>

100
00:06:54,255 --> 00:06:56,616
<font color="#FFFFFF">in-domain and under distribution shift,</font>

101
00:06:56,696 --> 00:07:02,937
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the effort it takes is controlled by how much
of the context shares the query's relevant pattern.</font>

102
00:07:03,017 --> 00:07:08,444
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trained model works by having attention focus
on the context examples that match the</font>

103
00:07:08,524 --> 00:07:12,637
<font color="#FFFFFF">query's relevant pattern while the ReLU MLP
amplifies their labels.</font>

104
00:07:12,717 --> 00:07:16,329
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because only the large-magnitude neurons carry this
signal,</font>

105
00:07:16,409 --> 00:07:19,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">pruning the small ones is essentially free,</font>

106
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:24,340
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving a principled reason why magnitude-based
pruning preserves in-context learning.</font>
